dsv41 patch 0078 (22.10.2026): Engram reads in prefill. 0078b in use from image v24 (VLLM_DSV41_ENGRAM_LOOKAHEAD=4096); 0078a off.

Base: image v23 (engram.py = image v4 = src_v12; model_runner.py = 0076 src). New file:
vllm/v1/worker/gpu/dsv41_engram_lookahead.py. All switches default off = v23 bit for bit (CPU test a).

What
  0078a VLLM_DSV41_ENGRAM_POOL_CPUS=0-4,10-13: the Engram disk reader's pread pool (256 threads a table, 2 tables a
        rank, created in EngramDiskTable's constructor) starts on the A725 cores instead of inheriting EXL3_CPU_PIN
        (5-9,15-19, the X925 cores of the worker's launch thread). The spin proxy thread keeps EXL3_CPU_PIN (it is
        created later by enable_spin). Pure Python: the creating thread holds the affinity for the constructor only.
  0078b VLLM_DSV41_ENGRAM_LOOKAHEAD=4096: once a prefill step's own Engram lookups have read their rows (CUDA event on
        the Engram prefetch stream), a background thread hashes the next 4096 prompt positions of every prefilling
        request on the host (numpy, same arithmetic as _hash_ids_kernel, token history from the runner's UVA
        all_token_ids) and reads their unique rows once through a second reader of each table (own EngramDiskTable:
        64 pread threads, no hot-row cache, no spin, on the A725 cores) into a scratch buffer. The next step's real
        lookup then finds them in the page cache. Ranges the real path has read meanwhile are skipped; a restart
        (preemption) resets the window. _THREADS / _CPUS / _BATCH / _LOG_S tune it; _VERIFY=1 compares the host hash
        of every processed chunk with the row ids the real lookup staged (pinned _disk_ids) and logs mismatches.
Why
  New text prefills 5-20 % slower than repeated text (KAMPAGNE 22.10. 01:05). A 4096-token chunk reads 49,152 rows a
  table and rank = ~98K random 4 KiB preads (weight + scale page), cold from the NVMe for new text. Layer 1 waits for
  its rows after layer 0 (~45-60 ms of cover); the recipe's reader bench gives ~20 ms per 16K cold rows at 256
  threads, so the read alone is ~60 ms, and buffered cold preads cost ~5-10 us CPU each: ~0.5-1 s CPU per chunk on the
  same 10 X925 cores as the launch thread, which the TensorFold prompt path syncs with every MoE layer (host read).
Expected effect
  0078b: chunks 2..n of a long prompt read warm rows (layer-1 wait and cold-read CPU leave the step); first chunk of a
  prompt unchanged (Hermes single-chunk turns: no gain). 0078a: less launch-thread starvation during the reads, the
  reads themselves on slower cores (layer-1 wait can grow on the first chunk) - only measurement decides.
  Target: cold prefill 32K/128K >= +3 %, warm +-1 %, decode +-1 %, numerics untouched (page cache only).
Measured quantities
  pf_engram.py cold (fresh pseudo-words, every n-gram new) vs warm (same text, new cache_salt) 32K/128K tok/s;
  0076a memprobe "eng" (serve ms per step of the main readers); the 0078 log line (rows read, ms per job, skipped
  ranges, verify 0 of N); decode_ab cells; t0_ident 8/8; first-token KL against the arm without the switch.
Test: v0078/test/test_engram_lookahead.py (CPU, Mac): runs the real _hash_ids_kernel source in a numpy emulation.

Measured (22.10.2026, image v24 at 1M with 0075/0076/0077/0081, spark2 at 2000 MHz, A/B on fresh starts, a repeat
of the reference at the end of the window moved decode -0.58 % and prefill +0.86 %, i.e. that is the noise):
  - fresh text against repeated text (new against warm Engram rows) differs by only 1.0 % (36K) / 0.2 % (150K) on the
    reference: cold reads are not the bottleneck the plan assumed;
  - 0078b alone (lookahead, main reader pool unchanged): prefill 131K-token prompts +9.4 % new / +8.6 % repeated
    text, 32K +8.3 % repeated, 478K-token needle +1.3 % (its sentences repeat, rows stay warm); decode -0.04 %;
    T=0 8/8; lowest free memory under the needle 3,023 against 2,470 MiB (the reference);
  - 0078a + 0078b (main pool on the A725 cores): prefill +9.9 % (131K) but every decode round +0.6..1.1 % slower
    (the decode-time Engram reads then run on the slower cores): not adopted;
  - main pool on all 20 cores + 0078b: prefill +2.7 %, decode +0.05 %: lower than 0078b alone.

